easywall (2.8.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Feat: a stolen password alone no longer opens the firewall. RFC 6238 TOTP
    for the single account, verified against the RFC's own published test
    vectors, plus eight one-time recovery codes stored argon2-hashed rather
    than in the clear. Setup lives on Password -> Second factor and nothing is
    written until a code confirms it. /login/verify becomes the second step of
    signing in whenever a factor is enrolled: the password step ends in an
    intermediate cookie, not a session, and issues no session until the code is
    right too. There is no reset link — clear totp_secret and recovery_codes in
    web.toml on the host, the same file the password already lives in
  * Feat: the first-run wizard offers a second factor too, unticked by default.
    Skipping is a first-class answer: POST /firstrun/skip creates the account
    with a password alone regardless of what the code field held, because
    easywall runs on boards with no RTC that come up at the epoch until NTP
    lands, and TOTP cannot verify against a clock like that
  * Feat: LOG_EVENT is the eighteenth command the socket protocol declares and
    the first one the web process sends rather than receives. Nine login events
    reach the audit log from a fixed enum rather than free text; the three a
    stranger can trigger without any credential are debounced in the core
  * Fix: an unauthenticated POST /logout could erase the visible audit log. The
    route is public and unlimited and recorded an event even for a request
    carrying no session, so a bare curl loop with no cookie pushed the whole
    200-line history out of view in under two seconds
  * Fix: the public demo could be locked. POST /password wrote credentials for
    real with no demo-mode test in any handler, so a visitor could overwrite the
    demo password and shut everyone else out until the process was restarted.
    Every credential-writing route now refuses in demo mode
  * Fix: the sidebar read "v2" — the major, not the version — and would have
    read v2 in 2.19 as well. A test now scans every shipped template for a
    version literal
  * Fix: Renovate found all seven Go toolchain pins and updated none of them.
    Two-component pins under npm versioning yield an empty candidate set, and
    one file pattern still named a page the Jekyll restructure had left behind
    as a redirect stub. The toolchain moves to 1.27.0
  * Changed: every session in flight ends once on upgrade — the credential
    fingerprint now covers the TOTP state as well as the password hash
  * See CHANGELOG.md for the complete account

